window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Quick Start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Your first working menu in about five minutes. You need Unity 6000.0 or newer. TextMesh Pro's Essential Resources must be in the project (Unity offers to import them the first time a text appears; you can also use Window > TextMeshPro > Import TMP Essential Resources)."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"1. Open the kit window","u":"getting-started.html#1-open-the-kit-window","x":"Tools > All In One Game Settings & Menu Kit > Open Window. It opens on the Home tab. (A short welcome window also appears once after import; you can turn it off.)"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"2. Follow the four Quick Start buttons","u":"getting-started.html#2-follow-the-four-quick-start-buttons","x":"Each step turns into a green check mark when it is done. Copy profile. Copies the kit's settings profile to Assets/AllInOneGameSettingsMenuData , so your edits never touch the kit's own files (updates stay safe). Create and assign. Creates a starter Audio Mixer (Master, Music, SFX, Voice, UI) and connects the volume sliders to it. Route your AudioSources to its groups. Master Volume works even without a mixer. Create front end. Adds the menu prefab to the open scene, plus an EventSystem if the scene has none. One Ctrl+Z undoes it. Add Menu Scene Mode. Marks this scene as your menu scene. In yo"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"3. Press Play","u":"getting-started.html#3-press-play","x":"Any key leaves the title screen. Open Settings , change something and press Apply . Changes are saved and come back the next time you play. Display changes ask \"Keep these display settings?\" and undo themselves after 15 seconds if you don't confirm. Settings, Display tab"},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"4. Connect your game","u":"getting-started.html#4-connect-your-game","x":"New Game raises MenuRoot.onNewGame : in the Inspector of the front end, add your scene-loading method there (or set New Game Scene to load a scene with a loading screen). Continue and Load Game raise their own events; tell the menu whether a save exists with MenuRoot.Instance.SetHasSave(true) . Want a different look? Theme tab > Use on Tactical or Storybook."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"The guided tour","u":"getting-started.html#the-guided-tour","x":"On the Home tab, Next walks through the other tabs in six short steps: Setup, Theme, Settings, Check and About."},{"p":"Quick Start","h":"Next","u":"getting-started.html#next","x":"Samples show the kit in a puzzle, a first-person and a cozy adventure game. Settings catalog explains how settings reach your game."},{"p":"Samples","h":"Samples","u":"samples.html#samples","x":"Open any scene in Samples/Scenes and press Play. Each one starts on its menu; New Game starts playing in the same scene, Esc / Start pauses. Every sample uses only what ships with the kit and works with Built-in, URP and HDRP, and with either input system. Scene Theme What it shows TryIt Clean The smallest setup: the front end and a spinning cube. Pause freezes time. Puzzle2D_Clean Clean A \"lights out\" puzzle (mouse, or arrows and Enter / gamepad). The board size follows Difficulty (a Read setting). Shooter3D_Tactical Tactical A first-person walk: mouse / controller sensitivity and invert (Rea"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Screens","u":"screens.html#screens","x":"The front end is one prefab ( Content/Prefabs/FrontEnd.prefab ) with every screen. One instance survives scene loads, and there is always exactly one EventSystem. Turn screens you don't need off in the kit window ( Setup tab > Screens ): they are removed when the game starts, and the buttons leading to them hide. Screen What it does Title Your game's name and \"Press any key\". Main menu Continue (only when a save exists), New Game, Load Game, Settings, Credits, Quit. Settings Tabs of settings, help text for the focused setting, Apply, Cancel, Reset Category, Reset All. Leaving with unapplied ch"},{"p":"Screens","h":"Scene modes","u":"screens.html#scene-modes","x":"Put a Menu Scene Mode component in each scene: Front End (menu scenes): the menu shows on load, optionally with the title screen. Gameplay (levels): the menu is hidden; Esc / Start pauses. Hidden : the menu stays out of the way (cutscenes, loading levels)."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Navigation","u":"screens.html#navigation","x":"Keyboard, mouse and gamepad work everywhere. Focus is always visible and comes back to the button you left when you go back. Back is Esc or the gamepad's east button."},{"p":"Screens","h":"Text","u":"screens.html#text","x":"Every text can be translated ( Languages ). Texts shrink to fit long translations instead of being cut off; the game title may take two lines."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Settings catalog","u":"settings-catalog.html#settings-catalog","x":"A Settings Profile lists the tabs (categories) and settings your menu shows. The kit ships a profile with the full launch catalog; add, remove, reorder or create settings in the kit window's Settings tab."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Tiers: how a setting reaches your game","u":"settings-catalog.html#tiers-how-a-setting-reaches-your-game","x":"Tier Meaning Examples Auto The kit applies it for you. Nothing to do. Resolution, VSync, quality, volumes, post effects, menu size, language Connect It drives something in your scene: a component (for example Camera Field Of View Target ), a Setting Event Binder with UnityEvents, or your callback. Field of view, your own custom settings Read The kit stores it; your code reads it when it needs it. Difficulty, camera shake, vibration, hold or toggle The badge next to each setting in the window shows its tier."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Tabs in the default profile","u":"settings-catalog.html#tabs-in-the-default-profile","x":"Display, Graphics, Audio, Controls, Accessibility, Gameplay. Settings that need something the project doesn't have (a render pipeline, the Input System, a mixer, a second monitor) are hidden automatically, with the reason shown in the catalog. See the Settings reference for every setting."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Add a setting from the catalog","u":"settings-catalog.html#add-a-setting-from-the-catalog","x":"Settings tab > Catalog : search, then Add . The kit copies the setting next to your profile (your copy, safe from updates). Remove takes it out again. The kit's own profile can't be edited: make an editable copy first (Home tab, step 1)."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Create your own setting","u":"settings-catalog.html#create-your-own-setting","x":"Settings tab > Custom setting (with UnityEvents) : choose Toggle, Slider, Whole number or Choice, give it an id (for example gameplay.hints ), a label and a tab, and click Create and add to profile . Then select a GameObject and click Add event binder to selection : its UnityEvents fire with the new value whenever the player applies settings."},{"p":"Settings catalog","h":"Needs connection","u":"settings-catalog.html#needs-connection","x":"The Check tab lists Connect settings that nothing in the open scene listens to yet (for example Field of View without a camera target), each with a one-click fix."},{"p":"Display safety","h":"Display safety","u":"display-safety.html#display-safety","x":"Changing resolution, refresh rate, window mode or monitor can leave a player with a black screen. The kit protects them: Confirmation: after a display change, \"Keep these display settings?\" counts down from 15 seconds. Without Keep , the previous mode comes back by itself. Crash recovery: the kit notes the pending change in the settings file before applying it. If the game crashes or is closed during the countdown, the next start restores the previous mode. First launch: the defaults are the mode the game started in, so nothing changes until the player chooses. Display change confirmation"},{"p":"Display safety","h":"The settings file","u":"display-safety.html#the-settings-file","x":"Saved as settings.json in Application.persistentDataPath (versioned JSON). Written safely: a temporary file is written first, and the previous file is kept as settings.json.bak . A damaged file is replaced by the backup or the defaults; the damaged copy is kept as settings.json.corrupt and one warning is logged. Start the game with -reset-settings to delete the saved settings (useful for support)."},{"p":"Display safety","h":"Other details","u":"display-safety.html#other-details","x":"Refresh rates are offered per resolution; a resolution change re-checks the refresh rate. Exclusive full screen is offered where the platform supports it. Monitor selection appears only on Windows with two or more monitors."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Audio","u":"audio.html#audio","x":""},{"p":"Audio","h":"Volumes","u":"audio.html#volumes","x":"Setting Needs a mixer? Master Volume No. Without a mixer it controls AudioListener.volume . Music, Sound Effects, Voice, Interface Yes: each controls an exposed parameter of your Audio Mixer. Volume settings without a mixer are hidden, and the Check tab offers to fix it."},{"p":"Audio","h":"The starter mixer","u":"audio.html#the-starter-mixer","x":"Home tab, step 2 (or Setup tab > Create starter mixer ) copies a mixer with the groups Master, Music, SFX, Voice and UI and the exposed parameters MasterVolume , MusicVolume , SfxVolume , VoiceVolume and UiVolume , then assigns it. Route your AudioSources to the matching groups. To use your own mixer, expose each group's volume (right-click the Volume field > Expose) and type the parameter name into the volume setting. Sliders map linearly to decibels the way players expect (0% is silent)."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Menu sounds","u":"audio.html#menu-sounds","x":"Each theme has move, submit, back, open, slider, toggle and error sounds (Kenney Interface Sounds, CC0). They play through the front end's UI Sound Player ; set its output to your UI mixer group so the Interface volume controls them. Menu sounds keep playing while the game is paused."},{"p":"Audio","h":"Pause","u":"audio.html#pause","x":"In Freeze pause mode the kit pauses AudioListener (game audio stops) and restores it exactly on resume. See Pause and multiplayer hooks ."},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Graphics","u":"graphics.html#graphics","x":"Graphics settings follow the render pipeline your project uses. Settings a pipeline can't use are hidden there automatically. Setting Built-in URP HDRP Quality preset Yes Yes Yes Texture quality, anisotropic filtering, shadow distance, level of detail Yes Yes Yes VSync, frame rate limit Yes Yes Yes MSAA, shadow quality, shadow resolution Yes URP's own versions No Render scale, upscaling filter, HDR No Yes No Post anti-aliasing (FXAA, SMAA, TAA) No Yes No Bloom, motion blur, depth of field, film grain, vignette, chromatic aberration (on/off), brightness With Post Processing v2 Yes No HDRP proje"},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Preset or override","u":"graphics.html#preset-or-override","x":"Each detail (texture quality, shadows ...) is Preset by default: it follows the chosen quality preset. Picking a value overrides the preset for that detail, and the override stays when the player changes the preset."},{"p":"Graphics","h":"Your project's assets are never changed","u":"graphics.html#your-projects-assets-are-never-changed","x":"URP: the kit applies render scale, MSAA, HDR, upscaling and shadow resolution to a runtime copy of your active URP asset; your asset file is untouched (a test checks the files are byte-identical before and after). Post effects and brightness: a kit-owned global Volume (priority 1000) with a runtime profile. \"On\" leaves your own volumes in charge; only \"Off\" adds an override. In the Editor , every quality level and frame rate the kit changed is put back when you leave Play mode. Graphics settings, Tactical theme"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Controls and rebinding","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#controls-and-rebinding","x":""},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Without the Input System package","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#without-the-input-system-package","x":"The menus work with the old Input Manager (keyboard and gamepad navigation, Esc / Start to pause). Mouse and controller sensitivity, invert and vibration are stored settings (Read tier) your input code reads. Key rebinding and controller prompts need the Input System package."},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"With the Input System package","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#with-the-input-system-package","x":"Rebinding, controller prompts and the look settings switch on by themselves. In the kit window's Settings tab, click the Key Bindings setting of your profile copy (or add it from the catalog) to select it. Set Actions to your Input Action Asset, and Action Maps to the maps players may rebind (for example Player ). Optional: Include Actions to show only some actions. Protected Actions are never offered and never swapped away, so players can't lose menu controls (Submit, Cancel, Navigate, Pause by default). Look Actions : your look action(s). Mouse / Controller Sensitivity and Invert X / Y are a"},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Controller prompts","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#controller-prompts","x":"Bindings show button pictures for keyboard and mouse, Xbox, PlayStation and Switch controllers (Kenney Input Prompts, CC0), and switch to the device the player used last. Generic controllers show the stick pictures and text for face buttons. The Glyph Map asset ( InputSystem/Content/DefaultGlyphMap ) maps control names to sprites: replace any sprite with your own."},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Vibration","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#vibration","x":"ControllerRumble.Play(low, high, seconds, this) vibrates the current gamepad, scaled by the Vibration and Vibration Strength settings."},{"p":"Controls and rebinding","h":"Pause action","u":"controls-and-rebinding.html#pause-action","x":"The Input System front end uses Esc / gamepad Start for pause, or your own action: Setup tab > 3. Pause action ."},{"p":"Themes","h":"Themes","u":"themes.html#themes","x":"One Theme asset styles the whole front end: colors, font, text sizes and style (uppercase, letter spacing), sizes and spacing, sprites (panels, controls, focus frame, slider, toggle), screen transition (fade, slide up, scale), focus animation and UI sounds. Theme Look For Clean Neutral dark, rounded, blue accent, fades Any game Tactical Dark, angular cut corners, corner-bracket focus, uppercase with letter spacing, hazard orange, slides in Shooters, action Storybook Warm parchment, very round double-edged panels, heart toggles, bouncy focus, scales in Fantasy, adventure, cozy Each has a high-c"},{"p":"Themes","h":"Switch themes","u":"themes.html#switch-themes","x":"Tools > All In One Game Settings & Menu Kit > Open Window > Theme : click Use on a theme. The front end in the open scene restyles immediately (undoable). Tick Preview high contrast to see the variant. At runtime: MenuRoot.Instance.SetTheme(theme) ."},{"p":"Themes","h":"Make your own","u":"themes.html#make-your-own","x":"Duplicate a theme asset and change it, or create one with Create > Romano Assets > Settings Menu > Theme. Sprites are white and tinted by the theme colors; 9-slice borders keep corners crisp at any size. To support High Contrast, duplicate your theme as its variant, set strong colors (text at least 4.5:1 against panels and controls) and assign it to High Contrast Variant ."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Accessibility","u":"accessibility.html#accessibility","x":"The default profile has an Accessibility tab. Every option works in the menu with no code; the ones your game needs to react to are available from code too. Setting What the menu does What your game can do Menu Size (75% to 150%) Scales the whole menu canvas. Read AccessibilityState.UiScale for your own HUD. Text Size (80% to 160%) Scales every menu text. Read AccessibilityState.TextScale . High Contrast Switches to the theme's high-contrast variant (text contrast at least 4.5:1, checked by a test). Read AccessibilityState.HighContrast . Reduce Motion No screen fades, slides or focus zoom; ins"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Subtitles","u":"accessibility.html#subtitles","x":"Drag Content/Prefabs/Subtitles.prefab into your first scene (or a scene that stays loaded). From your dialogue code: SubtitleDisplay.Show(\"Where is everyone?\", 3f, \"Mara\"); Lines queue and play in order, in unscaled time (they keep going while paused). The player's Subtitles, Subtitle Size and Subtitle Background settings apply automatically."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Keyboard, controller and mouse","u":"accessibility.html#keyboard-controller-and-mouse","x":"Every screen is fully usable with a keyboard or a controller; focus is always visible and is restored when you go back. Rebinding protects the menu controls, so a player can't lock themselves out."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Not supported: screen readers","u":"accessibility.html#not-supported-screen-readers","x":"The menus are not read aloud by screen readers (Narrator, NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack). Unity's uGUI has no built-in screen reader support, and the kit does not add one. If your game needs a screen reader, plan for a dedicated solution."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Languages","u":"languages.html#languages","x":"Every string the menu shows has a key ( ui.… , setting.<id>.name , setting.<id>.help , setting.<id>.option.<value> , category.<id> , input.action.<name> ). The kit ships an English String Table ( Content/Localization/English.asset )."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Add a language","u":"languages.html#add-a-language","x":"Duplicate English.asset , rename it (e.g. German.asset ), and set Language Code ( de ) and Native Name ( Deutsch ). Translate the values (keep the keys and any {0} placeholders or <b> tags). Add the table to the Language setting (in your profile copy). Missing keys fall back to English. The default language is the player's system language when a table matches it, otherwise English."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Pseudo-localization (testing)","u":"languages.html#pseudo-localization-testing","x":"In the Editor and in development builds the Language setting also offers Pseudo : every text gets accented letters, 40% more length and brackets ( [Ñéŵ Ĝáɱé~~~~] ). Use it to find text that is cut off or not translated. Menu texts shrink to fit long translations; an automated test checks every screen at 4:3, 16:10, 16:9, 21:9 and 32:9, at 75%, 100% and 150% menu size, in every theme, with pseudo-localization on."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Fonts for other scripts (Chinese, Japanese, Korean, …)","u":"languages.html#fonts-for-other-scripts-chinese-japanese-korean-","x":"The included Inter font covers Latin scripts (Western European languages). For Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic or other scripts, create a TextMesh Pro font asset from a font that covers them (for example Noto Sans CJK, SIL OFL) and add it to the Fallback Font Assets list of the theme's font asset (or set it as the theme's font). Dynamic atlas population keeps the build small. Right-to-left languages need extra work in TextMesh Pro and are not handled by the kit."},{"p":"Languages","h":"Unity Localization package (optional)","u":"languages.html#unity-localization-package-optional","x":"When com.unity.localization is installed, the menu also asks a Unity Localization string table named SettingsMenuKit (same keys), and follows the package's selected locale. The bridge is compiled only with the package (1.4 or newer). A text missing from the selected locale's table comes from the English Unity table, then the kit's English table, then the text in the prefab. Change the table name with UnityLocalizationStringSource.TableName , or stop following the selected locale with UnityLocalizationStringSource.FollowSelectedLocale = false . Credits and loading tips come from your own text f"},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","u":"pause-and-multiplayer.html#pause-and-multiplayer-hooks","x":"Choose the Pause Mode on the front end (the MenuRoot component): Mode What happens Use it for Freeze Time.timeScale = 0 and (optionally) AudioListener.pause , both restored exactly on resume; the input lock is raised. Single-player games Overlay Only The pause menu shows over the running game; time doesn't stop; the input lock is raised. Multiplayer and online games, where time can't stop Custom Only the onPaused / onResumed events; your game decides everything. Your own pause logic"},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Input lock","u":"pause-and-multiplayer.html#input-lock","x":"While a menu should block gameplay input, MenuRoot.InputLocked is true and MenuRoot.InputLockChanged (static event) and onInputLockChanged (UnityEvent) fire. Disable your player input there: void OnEnable() => MenuRoot.InputLockChanged += OnLock; void OnDisable() => MenuRoot.InputLockChanged -= OnLock; void OnLock(bool locked) => playerInput.enabled = !locked;"},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Events","u":"pause-and-multiplayer.html#events","x":"onNewGame , onContinue , onLoadGame , onMainMenuRequested , onQuitRequested , onPaused , onResumed , onLoadCompleted , onInputLockChanged : all UnityEvents on the front end."},{"p":"Pause and multiplayer hooks","h":"Multiplayer notes","u":"pause-and-multiplayer.html#multiplayer-notes","x":"Settings are local to each player's machine (one settings file per user); nothing goes over the network. Use Overlay Only (or Custom) so pausing never stops a networked simulation. Pause On Controller Disconnect can be turned off for online games."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Extending and API","u":"extending.html#extending-and-api","x":"Everything is in the RomanoAssets.SettingsMenu namespaces. The kit's public API won't break in 1.x updates (an automated test checks every public type, member, serialized field, script GUID, menu item and setting id against the released version)."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Read a setting","u":"extending.html#read-a-setting","x":"using RomanoAssets.SettingsMenu; var settings = SettingsRuntime.Current; // created by the front end or a Settings Bootstrap float shake = settings.GetFloat(\"gameplay.camera-shake\", 1f); bool hints = settings.GetBool(\"gameplay.hints\"); string difficulty = settings.GetValue(\"gameplay.difficulty\"); Ids are listed in the Settings reference ."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"React when settings change","u":"extending.html#react-when-settings-change","x":"// Connect tier: called now and after every Apply that changes it. SettingsRuntime.Current.Connect(\"gameplay.field-of-view\", v => cam.fieldOfView = float.Parse(v, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)); // Any tier: an event per applied setting. SettingsRuntime.Current.ValueApplied += d => Debug.Log($\"{d.Id} is now {SettingsRuntime.Current.GetValue(d.Id)}\"); No code: add a Setting Event Binder component (UnityEvents for float, int, bool and string values)."},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"A connector component","u":"extending.html#a-connector-component","x":"public sealed class LightIntensityTarget : SettingConnector { [SerializeField] Light target; protected override void OnValue(string value) => target.intensity = float.Parse(value, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture); }"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Your own Auto setting","u":"extending.html#your-own-auto-setting","x":"Derive a definition (for example from FloatSettingDefinition ) and an applier, and register the applier once: public sealed class GammaApplier : SettingApplier { public override bool Handles(SettingDefinition d) => d is GammaSettingDefinition; public override void Apply(SettingsController c, IReadOnlyList<SettingDefinition> changed) { foreach (var d in changed) MyPostStack.SetGamma(c.GetFloat(d.Id, 1f)); } } [RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod(RuntimeInitializeLoadType.AfterAssembliesLoaded)] static void Register() => SettingsAppliers.Register(() => new GammaApplier());"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Change settings from code","u":"extending.html#change-settings-from-code","x":"var c = SettingsRuntime.Current; c.SetPendingFloat(\"audio.music-volume\", 0.5f); c.Apply(); // or c.Cancel()"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"The menu","u":"extending.html#the-menu","x":"var menu = MenuRoot.Instance; menu.SetHasSave(true); // shows Continue menu.EnterGameplay(); // hide menus; Esc / Start pauses menu.ShowFrontEnd(); // back to the main menu menu.Pause(); menu.Resume(); menu.SetTheme(myTheme); menu.LoadScene(\"Level1\", MenuMode.Gameplay); // with the loading screen"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Accessibility, subtitles, languages","u":"extending.html#accessibility-subtitles-languages","x":"SubtitleDisplay.Show(\"Over here!\", 3f, \"Mara\"); float shake = AccessibilityState.CameraShakeScale; // 0 with Reduce Motion bool reduced = AccessibilityState.ReducedMotion; string label = MenuLocalization.Get(\"mygame.hud.ammo\", \"Ammo\");"},{"p":"Extending and API","h":"Optional integrations","u":"extending.html#optional-integrations","x":"URP, the Input System, Post Processing v2, Cinemachine and Unity's Localization package are detected with assembly versionDefines : their code compiles only when the package is installed, and nothing is required."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"upgrading.html#100","x":"The first release. Nothing to do."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"How updates work","u":"upgrading.html#how-updates-work","x":"Your profile, settings, mixer and themes copies live in Assets/AllInOneGameSettingsMenuData (or wherever you saved them), outside the kit's folder: an update never overwrites them. Back up your project (or commit it) before importing an update, then import the new package over the old one. Public API, serialized fields, script GUIDs, menu paths and setting ids are kept across 1.x versions (an automated test checks them against the released version), so your scenes, prefabs and code keep working. A .unitypackage update never deletes files: files the kit no longer needs are left as harmless stub"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Settings reference","u":"settings-reference.html#settings-reference","x":"Every setting in the default profile, by tab. Tier : Auto = the kit applies it; Connect = it drives something in your scene (a component or your callback); Read = the kit stores it and your code reads it. Ids are what your code uses ( SettingsRuntime.Current.GetValue(\"id\") ) and what the settings file stores. Settings that need a package or a render pipeline are hidden where they can't work. This page is generated from the shipped settings."},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Display","u":"settings-reference.html#display","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Resolution display.resolution Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Screen resolution. Refresh Rate display.refresh-rate Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Refresh rate for the chosen resolution (exclusive full screen). Window Mode display.window-mode Auto the game's launch value Fullscreen, Borderless, Windowed Full screen, borderless or windowed. Monitor display.monitor Auto the game's launch value what the player's screen supports Which monitor shows the game. Only shown when more than one is co"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Graphics","u":"settings-reference.html#graphics","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Quality Preset graphics.quality Auto the game's launch value your project's quality levels Overall graphics quality. The options below can override parts of it. Texture Quality graphics.texture-quality Auto Preset Preset, Full, Half, Quarter, Eighth Texture resolution. Lower values use less video memory. Anisotropic Filtering graphics.anisotropic Auto Preset Preset, Off, Per texture, Forced on Sharpens textures seen at steep angles. Shadow Distance graphics.shadow-distance Auto Preset Preset, 25 m, 50 m, 100 m, 150 m, 200 m How far away shadows are d"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Audio","u":"settings-reference.html#audio","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Master Volume audio.master-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Overall volume. Music audio.music-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of music. Sound Effects audio.sfx-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of sound effects. Voice audio.voice-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of voice. Interface audio.ui-volume Auto 100% 0% to 100% Volume of interface."},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Controls","u":"settings-reference.html#controls","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Mouse Sensitivity controls.mouse-sensitivity Auto 1.0 0.1 to 5.0 How fast the camera turns. Controller Sensitivity controls.gamepad-sensitivity Auto 1.0 0.1 to 5.0 How fast the camera turns. Invert Horizontal Look controls.invert-x Auto Off On, Off Reverses the look direction on this axis. Invert Vertical Look controls.invert-y Auto Off On, Off Reverses the look direction on this axis. Vibration controls.vibration Read On On, Off Controller vibration. Vibration Strength controls.vibration-strength Read 100% 0% to 100% How strong controller vibration "},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Accessibility","u":"settings-reference.html#accessibility","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Menu Size accessibility.ui-scale Auto 100% 75% to 150% Makes the whole menu bigger or smaller. Text Size accessibility.text-size Auto 100% 80% to 160% Makes menu text bigger or smaller. High Contrast accessibility.high-contrast Auto Off On, Off Stronger colors and outlines for easier reading. Reduce Motion accessibility.reduced-motion Auto Off On, Off Turns off menu animations and camera shake. Focus Outline accessibility.focus-indicator Auto Normal Thin, Normal, Thick How thick the outline around the selected item is. Sprint accessibility.sprint-mod"},{"p":"Settings reference","h":"Gameplay","u":"settings-reference.html#gameplay","x":"Setting Id Tier Default Values What it does Language gameplay.language Auto English English, Pseudo Language of the menus. Difficulty gameplay.difficulty Read Normal Easy, Normal, Hard How challenging the game is. Your game reads it (Read tier). Field of View gameplay.field-of-view Connect 70 50 to 110 Camera field of view in degrees. Camera Shake gameplay.camera-shake Read 100% 0% to 100% Strength of camera shake effects."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-faq","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The menu doesn't react to the keyboard or gamepad","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-menu-doesnt-react-to-the-keyboard-or-gamepad","x":"The scene needs one EventSystem. The front end creates one when the scene has none. The kit window's Check tab finds extra EventSystems and wrong input modules, with a Fix button. Player Settings > Active Input Handling: the kit works with Input Manager (Old) , Input System Package (New) or Both . With \"New\" you need the Input System package installed."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Text is invisible or shows squares","u":"troubleshooting.html#text-is-invisible-or-shows-squares","x":"Import TMP Essential Resources (Window > TextMeshPro > Import TMP Essential Resources). For other alphabets, see Languages ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Music, Sound Effects, Voice or Interface volume doesn't show in Settings","u":"troubleshooting.html#music-sound-effects-voice-or-interface-volume-doesnt-show-in-settings","x":"These need an Audio Mixer: kit window > Home , step 2 (or Setup > Create starter mixer, then Assign). Master Volume works without one."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"A graphics setting is missing","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-graphics-setting-is-missing","x":"It doesn't apply to your render pipeline or project (for example URP settings in a Built-in project, or Monitor with one screen). The Settings tab's catalog shows why each hidden setting is hidden."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Key Bindings doesn't show","u":"troubleshooting.html#key-bindings-doesnt-show","x":"It needs the Input System package and your Input Action Asset in the Key Bindings setting ( Controls and rebinding )."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The display change reverted by itself","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-display-change-reverted-by-itself","x":"That's the 15-second safety countdown: choose Keep to keep a new display mode ( Display safety )."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Settings were reset","u":"troubleshooting.html#settings-were-reset","x":"A damaged settings file is replaced by its backup or the defaults (the damaged copy is kept as settings.json.corrupt ). Starting the game with -reset-settings deletes the saved settings on purpose."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"My quality settings changed after Play mode","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-quality-settings-changed-after-play-mode","x":"They shouldn't: the kit puts every quality value back when Play mode ends. If you see a change, please report it with your system info."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Still stuck?","u":"troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","x":"Kit window > About > Copy system info , then post it with your question: Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Bug tracker: https://github.com/marklromano/RomanoAssetSupport Website: https://marklromano.github.io"},{"p":"Known issues and limits","h":"Known issues and limits","u":"known-issues.html#known-issues-and-limits","x":"Screen readers (Narrator, NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack) are not supported: Unity's uGUI has no screen reader support. HDRP: the menus and all engine-wide settings work; HDRP-specific graphics settings are not included in 1.0. Right-to-left languages (Arabic, Hebrew) are not handled; Chinese, Japanese and Korean need a font asset that covers them ( Languages ). Monitor selection is available on Windows only (Unity's API). Mobile and consoles: the menus are safe-area aware and controller-friendly, but 1.0 was tested on Windows (Mono and IL2CPP players) and with a WebGL script build; Android and iOS"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Need help, found a bug, or have an idea? Here is where to go: Discord (questions, help, feature requests): https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Post in #help or #feature-requests and pick the All In One Game Settings & Menu Kit tag. Bug tracker (confirmed bugs): https://github.com/marklromano/RomanoAssetSupport Use the bug report form and choose this kit. Website: https://marklromano.github.io Online manual: https://marklromano.github.io/settings-menu-kit/docs/ Email: marklromano@outlook.com"},{"p":"Support","h":"What to include in a report","u":"support.html#what-to-include-in-a-report","x":"Open the kit window's About tab and click Copy system info , then paste it into your report. It contains: Kit version (e.g. 1.0.0) Unity version (e.g. 6000.3.24f1) Render pipeline: Built-in, URP or HDRP Input backend and the platform you build for Please add what you did, what you expected, what happened, and any Console errors. Replies usually come within a few days."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to the All In One Game Settings & Menu Kit are listed here, newest first. The format follows Keep a Changelog , and the kit uses semantic versioning."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - not released yet (release candidate)","u":"changelog.html#100---not-released-yet-release-candidate","x":"The first release."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added","x":"Front end in one prefab ( FrontEnd ): title screen, main menu, settings, credits, pause menu, loading screen, confirmation dialog, display-change countdown and controller-disconnected prompt. One instance survives scene loads, and there is always exactly one EventSystem. Settings with Apply / Cancel / Reset category / Reset all, an \"Unsaved changes\" prompt, and a crash-safe settings file (backup and corrupt-file recovery). Audio: master volume (works without a mixer) plus music, sound effects, voice and interface volumes through an optional starter Audio Mixer. Display: resolution, refresh rat"},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Upgrade notes","u":"changelog.html#upgrade-notes","x":"First release: nothing to do."}];
